/*
RMSTEST_42 - what does ignore_terrain_restrictions do with NO frame attribute
Feeds: objects.ts's whole-command gate, validate()'s RMS0315, preview-design:542

THIS HEADER ONCE VOIDED ITS OWN RUN. The shore-fish constant was written out in
full below and the map generated blank. The reason is one number: /* resolves to
token id 69 and that object is 69, so the word opens a nested comment and the
rest of the file is never read. See RMSTEST_56 and parser-design Sec.2.1.

The other constants in this header - the salmon, the olive tree, the mangrove
shallow - are fine, and not merely by observation: their numbers are not 69.

QUESTION
Namatjira was run in game on 2026-08-10 and its one shore-fish command spawns
nothing. That command is

  create_object SHORE _FISH {
    terrain_to_place_on DLC_MANGROVESHALLOW
    ignore_terrain_restrictions
    ...                       <- no set_place_for_every_player,
  }                              no place_on_specific_land_id

and guide:2509 carries "Requires: set_place_for_every_player or
place_on_specific_land_id". So the rule was taken to be: requirement unmet, the
whole command places nothing. That is what objects.ts and RMS0315 now implement.

THE PROBLEM IS THAT NAMATJIRA CANNOT TELL THE TWO MODELS APART, and both predict
the zero that was observed.

  Model A - the command is VOIDED. Requirement unmet, nothing places, whatever
            else the command says.
  Model B - the FLAG is merely INERT (which is what preview-design:542 already
            says for this whole attribute family). The flag does nothing, so
            the fish keeps its shore habitat, which is open water beside a
            beach. The command also says terrain_to_place_on
            DLC_MANGROVESHALLOW, and a shallow is never open water. The two
            constraints contradict, and zero fish place for that reason instead.

Both give zero shore fish on Namatjira. The map is silent on which.

WHY THIS MATTERS ENOUGH TO SPEND A RUN ON
Model A empties 47 commands across 11 corpus maps. Model B leaves most of them
placing objects normally and only removes the flag's effect. That is a large
difference in what the preview draws, and a large difference in whether RMS0315
is a warning about a dead command or a note about a dead attribute.

There is also evidence pointing at B, and it is worth stating because it argues
against the thing this project just shipped. find_closest carries the IDENTICAL
"Requires:" line at guide:2649, and the corpus uses it with no frame attribute
71 times across working maps. If that line meant "the command places nothing",
71 shipped commands would silently place nothing - and unlike a decorative fish,
missing starting resources is something an author would have noticed. So the
Requires line by itself does not appear to void a command, and if
ignore_terrain_restrictions really does, it is special rather than an instance
of a general rule.

METHOD
Three commands on one map, each with NO frame attribute, differing only in the
flag and in what the object is restricted from. The whole reading is the object
histogram plus where the objects sit.

The map is half grass, half water, so "on land" and "in water" are both
available and distinguishable in the probe.

  1. SALMON + ignore_terrain_restrictions
       A: nothing at all.
       B: salmon in the water only - the flag is inert, so the fish's own
          restriction still applies.
       C: salmon on the grass too - the flag works frameless, and neither A nor
          B is right.
  2. SALMON with no flag. The control. Salmon in the water under every model;
     if this one is empty the map itself is wrong and the run tells us nothing.
  3. OLIVE_TREE + ignore_terrain_restrictions. A land object with a flag that
     has nothing to lift, which separates "the command is voided" from anything
     to do with terrain.
       A: nothing.
       B and C: trees on the grass, normally.

Command 3 is the cleanest single discriminator and 1 is the informative one.
Read both.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), any player count.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --clusters
  Object counts are the answer. SALMON_1 / SALMON_2 cannot be told apart in the
  histogram, so read the total against 60 and use the water/land split: model B
  gives 60 (all in water), model A gives 30 (only the unflagged command), and
  model C gives 60 with some on grass.

ONE RUN IS ENOUGH. Every outcome here is a presence/absence at a factor of two,
not a distribution.

IF THE ANSWER IS B, revert the whole-command gate in objects.ts to a
flag-is-inert model and re-scope RMS0315 from "this command places nothing" to
"this attribute does nothing here". The 47-command corpus figure in the build
log becomes wrong and should be struck rather than left to age.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain WATER

  create_land
  {
    terrain_type GRASS
    land_percent 50
    left_border 0
    right_border 50
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

  /* 1. THE TEST. Flag, no frame attribute. */
  create_object SALMON
  {
    number_of_objects 30
    set_gaia_object_only
    ignore_terrain_restrictions
  }

  /* 2. CONTROL. Same object, no flag. Must produce fish in the water. */
  create_object SALMON
  {
    number_of_objects 30
    set_gaia_object_only
  }

  /* 3. THE CLEAN DISCRIMINATOR. A land object, so the flag has nothing to
     lift and only the voiding question is left. */
  create_object OLIVE_TREE
  {
    number_of_objects 30
    set_gaia_object_only
    ignore_terrain_restrictions
  }

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>
